  data.profile={text:'You are a decisive, analytical thinker who builds strong cases for the options you favour \u2014 and you know it. Your cases-for are consistently richer and more detailed than your cases-against, which suggests you arrive at most decisions with a position already forming and then use the process to validate it rather than genuinely test it. That said, when the process does challenge you, you respond well: you changed your mind on the Meridian offer when the challenge exposed that a number was doing most of your thinking.\n\nSTRENGTHS: Your pre-mortems are unusually specific and vivid. You don\u2019t write generic failure stories \u2014 you write scenarios with named people and concrete consequences. That\u2019s a genuine skill. Your friend test answers are consistently more honest than your direct analysis, which means you have strong judgment that you sometimes override with personal attachment.\n\nBLIND SPOTS: You avoid hard conversations. Three of your decisions involve a conversation you haven\u2019t had yet \u2014 with your wife, with Sarah, with Marcus. You tend to make decisions ABOUT people without talking TO them first. You also consistently undervalue your own sunk cost bias: you name it in every analysis but it still influences your initial leaning.\n\nPATTERNS: Numbers anchor you heavily. The 40% salary, the $80K credits, the 10x market size \u2014 in each case a headline number pulled you toward a decision before you\u2019d examined the substance. Your debrief lessons circle the same theme: don\u2019t let a single compelling data point substitute for a full picture.\n\nFOR NEXT TIME: 1) Write your case-against FIRST, before the case-for. 2) Before any decision involving another person, have the conversation with them before you decide. 3) When a number is the main argument, set it aside and ask: would I still lean this way without it?',generatedAt:now-5*DAY,decisionCount:10};
